  'Food Science',
  'Food Science',
  'This course examines how food composition, molecular structure, physical properties, microorganisms, processing, packaging, and storage determine safety, nutritional quality, sensory properties, functionality, and shelf life. Students study water, carbohydrates, proteins, enzymes, lipids, micronutrients, pigments, flavors, colloids, rheology, additives, thermal and nonthermal processing, fermentation, preservation, packaging, sensory science, quality assurance, regulation, and sustainability. Laboratory work emphasizes safe technique, standardized preparation, analytical measurement, experimental design, statistics, scientific writing, and an evidence-based product-development project relevant to nutrition and dietetics.',
  $fscijson${"slug": "food-science", "title": "Food Science", "description": "This course examines how food composition, molecular structure, physical properties, microorganisms, processing, packaging, and storage determine safety, nutritional quality, sensory properties, functionality, and shelf life. Students study water, carbohydrates, proteins, enzymes, lipids, micronutrients, pigments, flavors, colloids, rheology, additives, thermal and nonthermal processing, fermentation, preservation, packaging, sensory science, quality assurance, regulation, and sustainability. Laboratory work emphasizes safe technique, standardized preparation, analytical measurement, experimental design, statistics, scientific writing, and an evidence-based product-development project relevant to nutrition and dietetics.", "topics": [{"id": "f756f463-c99b-40e2-b218-b461961fabc6", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Science Foundations and Experimental Thinking", "description": "This opening section defines food science as an interdisciplinary study of composition, structure, processing, safety, quality, nutrition, and consumer experience. Students learn to ask testable questions and connect molecular change to measurable food behavior.", "children": [{"id": "f20c6c79-6df9-41c5-9f49-3c6cf96e78a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Food as a complex system", "description": "Students move beyond ingredient lists to see foods as dynamic biological and physical systems.", "children": [{"id": "989f0e6d-7be2-41c8-8e96-e11b01015ca2", "type": "concept", "title": "Food matrices, phases, interfaces, structure, and hierarchy"}, {"id": "969b43fd-9341-48c2-9750-3ff8561f54cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Composition–structure–process–property relationships"}, {"id": "e027e743-3bfb-404c-b0d5-4f52345f8a73", "type": "concept", "title": "Raw materials, formulation, processing, storage, preparation, digestion, and consumption"}]}, {"id": "d4f70548-eb61-4cea-89a9-39f23b592913", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scientific method and evidence", "description": "This subtopic establishes how food scientists design comparisons, control variation, and make claims supported by data.", "children": [{"id": "4a366049-0a46-4fe8-b107-1109494aace1", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question, hypothesis, mechanism, prediction, and operational definition"}, {"id": "11892c05-8427-431c-ad59-fee1fe7686db", "type": "concept", "title": "Independent, dependent, controlled, confounding, and nuisance variables"}, {"id": "dd487de3-60ea-4009-a4dc-304a63836795", "type": "concept", "title": "Replication, randomization, controls, blinding, uncertainty, reproducibility, and causal inference"}]}, {"id": "563c422d-7e78-4134-9e21-3e977d7487de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Laboratory safety and scientific integrity", "description": "Students learn to work safely and document methods and data so another person can understand and repeat the work.", "children": [{"id": "e177dc12-92c4-4701-b2a1-203802e1eb70", "type": "concept", "title": "Chemical, thermal, electrical, mechanical, pressure, sharp, allergen, and biological hazards"}, {"id": "9258a695-40de-49ff-8c03-0d77a817f3d0", "type": "concept", "title": "PPE, safety data sheets, risk assessment, waste, equipment training, and emergency response"}, {"id": "faea5edd-4fb3-4541-bbe6-f06cc432521b", "type": "concept", "title": "Laboratory notebook, raw data, units, significant figures, version control, attribution, and no fabrication"}]}]}, {"id": "a87cafb1-8825-4bf4-9683-c0f65bdabc5d", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Composition and Analytical Measurement", "description": "Composition data underpins nutrition, labeling, formulation, quality, and research. This section teaches students what common measurements mean, how they are obtained, and where error enters.", "children": [{"id": "e8704953-e67a-4c08-9add-8e307a7d6b48", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Proximate and component analysis", "description": "Students learn the logic and limitations of measuring moisture, ash, protein, lipid, carbohydrate, fiber, and energy.", "children": [{"id": "13da0ee7-ddba-48fa-ab95-04f29650cea1", "type": "concept", "title": "Direct measurement versus calculation by difference"}, {"id": "f1a66cfd-8dfb-4540-886e-aab88988e73e", "type": "concept", "title": "Kjeldahl/Dumas concepts, solvent extraction, gravimetry, ashing, and dietary-fiber methods"}, {"id": "b1fbac74-d948-4b7e-b6a1-5801a35c3591", "type": "concept", "title": "Wet basis, dry basis, conversion factors, recovery, and matrix effects"}]}, {"id": "c5e0a228-5ae0-4e38-8324-4439789a7cc7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sampling and sample preparation", "description": "This subtopic explains why a precise instrument cannot rescue an unrepresentative sample.", "children": [{"id": "ad5aaf00-3846-4876-855a-90fc96d91559", "type": "concept", "title": "Population, lot, increment, composite, laboratory sample, and analytical portion"}, {"id": "d32a9a1f-086e-4cb1-b16f-e841f351e043", "type": "concept", "title": "Homogenization, grinding, subsampling, storage, preservation, and contamination prevention"}, {"id": "ccc5f7b2-3c84-4b78-82dd-2ef2f9624264", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling error, heterogeneity, stratification, and chain of custody"}]}, {"id": "4d471b3f-d2dc-4a13-a7aa-57502b2dba96", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Method quality and data interpretation", "description": "Students evaluate whether a method is fit for purpose and whether a difference is meaningful.", "children": [{"id": "fd677266-5478-45bb-89ae-30b06df80d4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Accuracy, precision, bias, sensitivity, selectivity, detection/quantitation limits, range, and robustness"}, {"id": "7f352046-e70a-4284-a6c0-2224b4d4cd48", "type": "concept", "title": "Calibration, blanks, standards, controls, spikes, duplicates, reference materials, and uncertainty"}, {"id": "c433e9ca-9f30-4f55-ace7-4e3ef8a76e4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Validated methods, quality-control charts, outliers, significant figures, and comparison with FoodData Central"}]}]}, {"id": "d791e86e-b525-4f16-b4eb-d19698d9901d", "type": "topic", "title": "Water, Moisture, and Water Activity", "description": "Water controls texture, mobility, reactions, microbial growth, freezing, and shelf life. Students learn why total moisture and available water are not the same concept.", "children": [{"id": "e9ff696f-e263-4963-bc13-2c3c0ed3ec5e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Molecular properties and states of water", "description": "Students connect hydrogen bonding and molecular mobility to food structure and processing behavior.", "children": [{"id": "f90ae109-4919-4329-91b6-a3581dbe8e91", "type": "concept", "title": "Free, bound, immobilized, and structural water"}, {"id": "9b9c0297-e58f-4aac-b787-c15a6716a54e", "type": "concept", "title": "Water as solvent, plasticizer, reactant, and heat-transfer medium"}, {"id": "7f498bbd-a4ad-4204-aa86-248a5ab7388a", "type": "concept", "title": "Ice formation, supercooling, glass transition, freeze concentration, and thawing"}]}, {"id": "b3832a14-b03c-4d28-bf25-798376589cf8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Moisture content versus water activity", "description": "This subtopic distinguishes the quantity of water from its thermodynamic availability.", "children": [{"id": "58d473d0-bbed-4e0e-bf8b-ec62c482b623", "type": "concept", "title": "Water activity definition and measurement concepts"}, {"id": "70eb16e5-af42-4bfd-8806-35cf65620a4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Microbial growth limits, reaction rates, texture, and ingredient interactions"}, {"id": "a14a5119-a4ce-4132-a6f4-afff1c705b8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Humectants, salt/sugar effects, temperature effects, and equilibrium relative humidity"}]}, {"id": "1901c258-fccc-41cc-ba9a-0bc1c1f76c9e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sorption and moisture control", "description": "Students use sorption behavior to reason about drying, packaging, caking, crispness, and stability.", "children": [{"id": "3d463118-7448-4105-81f5-20277035684e", "type": "concept", "title": "Adsorption/desorption isotherms and hysteresis"}, {"id": "a40737c2-9c4d-473e-a72e-4d1c4fabecd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Monolayer region, capillary condensation, and critical moisture"}, {"id": "40fc8e51-16cd-4a57-956c-8bf0264031d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Drying, concentration, moisture migration, barrier packaging, and shelf-life prediction"}]}]}, {"id": "62c4d059-f66f-4847-b581-f7ef3a9a812d", "type": "topic", "title": "Carbohydrates in Foods", "description": "Carbohydrates provide sweetness, bulk, structure, viscosity, gelation, browning, and dietary fiber. This section links molecular structure to culinary, industrial, and nutritional function.", "children": [{"id": "9df47153-5e71-45fe-bea3-2876d0494c01", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sugars and small carbohydrates", "description": "Students examine how structure affects sweetness, solubility, crystallization, water binding, and chemical reactivity.", "children": [{"id": "dac7fe0b-697b-4584-b3e3-cf19dda4a33e", "type": "concept", "title": "Monosaccharides, disaccharides, oligosaccharides, reducing sugars, and sugar alcohols"}, {"id": "e2f64e71-d298-4786-ad10-3491db513627", "type": "concept", "title": "Sweetness, hygroscopicity, humectancy, colligative effects, inversion, and crystallization"}, {"id": "a9a62d7e-26a3-4dd4-8368-11dc2bccd0d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Caramelization and Maillard participation"}]}, {"id": "42cd1290-389a-4e37-a9a0-af3afb81f6f5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Starch and hydrocolloids", "description": "This subtopic explains thickening, gelation, stability, and texture across sauces, baked goods, and processed foods.", "children": [{"id": "bc1c6d27-edd6-406c-85d1-643f3de99220", "type": "concept", "title": "Amylose, amylopectin, granules, gelatinization, pasting, gelation, retrogradation, and syneresis"}, {"id": "fb16530a-81ab-47f7-998d-000ab2a98407", "type": "concept", "title": "Modified starches, gums, pectins, cellulose derivatives, alginates, and carrageenans"}, {"id": "499dc0f3-0b82-48c4-8948-c16221771fc0", "type": "concept", "title": "Viscosity, suspension, water binding, freeze–thaw stability, and process tolerance"}]}, {"id": "2a7a98f5-cecd-4c68-ac36-373f2069e91f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dietary fiber and carbohydrate quality", "description": "Students connect physicochemical properties to digestion, glycemic response, fermentation, satiety, and formulation.", "children": [{"id": "f724907f-f8ea-478c-9b8a-df963b8461f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Soluble/insoluble, viscous/nonviscous, fermentable/nonfermentable classifications"}, {"id": "5428cb9e-58ac-44a6-b5a6-413f03e05625", "type": "concept", "title": "Resistant starch, beta-glucans, inulin/fructans, pectins, and cell-wall matrices"}, {"id": "88ff35b9-b6fa-4c08-bec5-5c62b90a3426", "type": "concept", "title": "Processing effects, particle size, food structure, analytical definitions, and nutrition claims"}]}]}, {"id": "0fb71ef8-a291-4a54-8873-97062ee407f8", "type": "topic", "title": "Proteins, Enzymes, and Protein Functionality", "description": "Proteins determine structure, water binding, foaming, emulsification, gelation, and nutrient quality. Enzymes drive desirable and undesirable transformations during processing and storage.", "children": [{"id": "99d0b1e8-8fef-490e-ba94-e39afec5b958", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Protein structure and stability", "description": "Students connect amino-acid sequence and molecular forces to folding, denaturation, aggregation, and digestibility.", "children": [{"id": "8bc6fcf9-d904-423c-9f5b-624cdb9f4443", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary through quaternary structure; globular, fibrous, and intrinsically disordered regions"}, {"id": "b3a34b74-14a6-4378-a8d8-1fdc64060f3d", "type": "concept", "title": "Hydrogen bonding, hydrophobic interactions, ionic forces, and disulfide bonds"}, {"id": "2f68ba21-b484-49eb-9ee1-8305aebad23d", "type": "concept", "title": "Heat, pH, salts, shear, solvents, oxidation, and pressure effects"}]}, {"id": "8e279286-4802-4580-bfac-1645ec24cb04", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Functional properties in foods", "description": "This subtopic explains how proteins create texture and stabilize air, water, and fat phases.", "children": [{"id": "b7d6f3f8-66c7-46f2-b787-3aad53f880ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Solubility, hydration, water-holding, swelling, viscosity, gelation, adhesion, and film formation"}, {"id": "236b51eb-332a-499d-a9ad-8c6564bf8112", "type": "concept", "title": "Foams and emulsions: adsorption, interfacial films, whipping, overrun, drainage, and coalescence"}, {"id": "48d26986-3afc-4fa7-b1ab-226f8090c945", "type": "concept", "title": "Gluten networks, egg coagulation, milk proteins, meat proteins, legumes, and plant-protein isolates"}]}, {"id": "95f92d9e-a6a2-442e-95c7-3793a9225c74", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Enzymes in preparation and processing", "description": "Students learn how enzymes can soften, brown, clarify, tenderize, coagulate, generate flavor, or destroy quality.", "children": [{"id": "94708de2-e026-452e-9e59-7d9fbd5c519e", "type": "concept", "title": "Catalysis, specificity, cofactors, rate, temperature, pH, inhibition, and inactivation"}, {"id": "db629b69-3df4-4148-87ed-292a8430bc51", "type": "concept", "title": "Polyphenol oxidase, pectinases, amylases, proteases, lipases, rennet, transglutaminase, and lactase"}, {"id": "bb309734-451d-4962-90aa-a47c7eb200a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Blanching, enzyme assays, immobilization concepts, and processing applications"}]}, {"id": "90732290-4baf-4967-8ed3-0f4c45090f5e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nutritional quality of proteins", "description": "Students relate food processing and matrix effects to amino-acid availability and protein quality.", "children": [{"id": "860743ec-337c-4613-b031-d11e9ae9d5c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Essential amino acids, limiting amino acid, digestibility, complementarity, and quality scores"}, {"id": "a8eebead-1166-4244-80e8-f4275ace8796", "type": "concept", "title": "Denaturation versus destruction; antinutrients and digestibility"}, {"id": "39e88482-ab46-490f-9485-6c8c21b49b83", "type": "concept", "title": "Maillard-related lysine loss, oxidation, heat damage, and processing tradeoffs"}]}]}, {"id": "5c4b97a9-d836-4c21-8144-880600f32efa", "type": "topic", "title": "Lipids in Foods", "description": "Lipids contribute energy, essential fatty acids, flavor, mouthfeel, structure, heat transfer, and bioactive delivery. This section connects composition and physical state to functionality and stability.", "children": [{"id": "af28e7e0-be28-472d-b3ff-6dde8ca2bb22", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Lipid classes and molecular structure", "description": "Students distinguish major lipid types and relate saturation and chain structure to properties.", "children": [{"id": "46df7bd1-ee2a-4d9f-8681-ad07ae652fa9", "type": "concept", "title": "Fatty acids, triacylglycerols, phospholipids, sterols, waxes, and fat-soluble compounds"}, {"id": "3283ffdb-eddb-482c-9d82-14f7db24c908", "type": "concept", "title": "Saturation, cis/trans configuration, chain length, melting, and polarity"}, {"id": "135756c3-8dba-41c4-a772-955486832cb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Essential fatty acids and nutritional context"}]}, {"id": "be8e07fa-a046-4a8e-a245-8ed8d557a0a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fat crystallization and functionality", "description": "This subtopic explains why fats can be plastic, spreadable, brittle, smooth, or grainy.", "children": [{"id": "3c7e9d2e-4627-41a2-8b2a-f2f8be8e5fcd", "type": "concept", "title": "Nucleation, crystal growth, polymorphism, solid-fat content, and tempering"}, {"id": "9ad2d820-b0d2-4761-9562-4f2767ee9ce1", "type": "concept", "title": "Shortening, aeration, creaming, lamination, chocolate snap/gloss, and fat bloom"}, {"id": "21a7deb1-8561-494c-a234-0aeb802708cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Interesterification, fractionation, blending, and replacement strategies"}]}, {"id": "67ee26f7-5f7f-434a-b351-2d432235c4cf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Emulsions and lipid oxidation", "description": "Students learn to stabilize oil/water systems and prevent rancidity.", "children": [{"id": "445be878-0be0-4184-beca-006d436ab4c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Oil-in-water, water-in-oil, multiple emulsions, emulsifiers, proteins, particles, and homogenization"}, {"id": "65ff7db5-1255-4280-b427-fd32ca78f343", "type": "concept", "title": "Creaming, sedimentation, flocculation, coalescence, Ostwald ripening, and phase inversion"}, {"id": "de4d91d1-4bac-4ff2-9607-83be3855ab50", "type": "concept", "title": "Autoxidation, photooxidation, hydrolysis, prooxidants, antioxidants, packaging, and sensory/nutritional consequences"}]}]}, {"id": "56efe8dd-233e-47a8-94de-dd9a2030b0e1", "type": "topic", "title": "Micronutrients, Pigments, Flavor, and Bioactive Compounds", "description": "Food quality and nutritional value depend on compounds present in small amounts. Students learn how processing, light, oxygen, pH, and matrix interactions change vitamins, minerals, color, aroma, taste, and bioactives.", "children": [{"id": "f01f4106-a76d-4b37-8ec3-7dcd1fa589b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Vitamins and minerals", "description": "Students evaluate stability, bioaccessibility, fortification, and analytical interpretation rather than assuming processing always lowers nutrition.", "children": [{"id": "382076a2-cc4e-48ed-a60d-6af51896e59c", "type": "concept", "title": "Water- and fat-soluble vitamin stability to heat, light, oxygen, pH, and leaching"}, {"id": "ee72f96b-a355-442d-997b-25ebdbdcac6a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mineral stability, oxidation state, solubility, interactions, and processing contamination"}, {"id": "ac4c7638-3d09-43b1-9c72-0300d9748003", "type": "concept", "title": "Fortification, enrichment, overages, premixes, uniformity, and bioavailability"}]}, {"id": "311aeed8-4866-4097-8809-ee5b9a2b8fbb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Natural pigments and color chemistry", "description": "This subtopic connects molecular structure and environment to visual quality.", "children": [{"id": "bd051eab-c963-47a9-ba09-18df9077767d", "type": "concept", "title": "Chlorophylls, carotenoids, anthocyanins, betalains, myoglobin, and melanins"}, {"id": "586650a3-4aa5-4e5d-8c77-3217de15d0dd", "type": "concept", "title": "pH, heat, oxygen, metals, enzymes, light, and packaging effects"}, {"id": "26cf9831-45c6-49fc-83ec-ceee4613977e", "type": "concept", "title": "Color measurement: CIELAB concepts, instruments, standards, and consumer perception"}]}, {"id": "90345954-a15e-463b-8702-628c74f1cb04", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Flavor chemistry and perception", "description": "Students study how volatile and nonvolatile compounds create taste, aroma, chemesthesis, and aftertaste.", "children": [{"id": "de68242f-31aa-446d-8d00-4814fa9fcfb9", "type": "concept", "title": "Basic tastes, aroma, retronasal olfaction, trigeminal sensations, and texture–flavor interactions"}, {"id": "c0fdc59a-2ae9-429a-a52b-47633a5f3212", "type": "concept", "title": "Flavor precursors and formation through Maillard chemistry, caramelization, fermentation, oxidation, and enzymatic reactions"}, {"id": "503e4697-eba6-46ac-b17a-b5f7f1996279", "type": "concept", "title": "Volatility, partitioning, release, binding, matrix effects, off-flavors, and flavor encapsulation"}]}, {"id": "8356c4e2-2a84-47c1-9aa6-4407abecf9c8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bioactives and antinutritional factors", "description": "Students critically assess claims about phytochemicals and processing effects.", "children": [{"id": "5dd06816-32ec-4d1e-aba7-013a25120d98", "type": "concept", "title": "Polyphenols, carotenoids, glucosinolates, phytosterols, and other candidate bioactives"}, {"id": "eacc6d45-dc12-4c6e-8479-08b0745eed59", "type": "concept", "title": "Phytate, oxalate, tannins, lectins, protease inhibitors, and cyanogenic compounds"}, {"id": "cd5e12e6-5a82-464a-a795-21c0ec533f9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Dose, matrix, bioaccessibility, metabolism, evidence quality, and safe processing"}]}]}, {"id": "55ebec3b-c5fa-40f7-99e0-174043fac969", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Physical Chemistry, Colloids, Texture, and Rheology", "description": "Many food qualities emerge from structure across scales rather than composition alone. This section teaches students to reason about dispersions, flow, deformation, and transitions.", "children": [{"id": "025a723f-98ba-4e8f-9a69-c8cbe813950b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Food dispersions and colloids", "description": "Students classify multiphase systems and predict instability mechanisms.", "children": [{"id": "e76e3b0d-d21e-444e-bfb8-7d8985662dff", "type": "concept", "title": "Solutions, suspensions, emulsions, foams, gels, sols, and complex multiphase foods"}, {"id": "344680a1-bcb3-4d8a-8c26-066bf9b2d937", "type": "concept", "title": "Particle/droplet size, surface area, interfacial tension, charge, steric stabilization, and density"}, {"id": "d82370ff-14e0-4f86-8d9b-ea9768fbb831", "type": "concept", "title": "Aggregation, sedimentation, creaming, coalescence, drainage, and phase separation"}]}, {"id": "612b4d8e-0691-48bb-b55a-157313f51c2f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rheology and texture", "description": "This subtopic links force and time to flow and deformation in liquids, semi-solids, and solids.", "children": [{"id": "cc764c46-8d46-4eb7-a686-e4c2896f8894", "type": "concept", "title": "Stress, strain, viscosity, elasticity, viscoelasticity, yield stress, and thixotropy"}, {"id": "7e0f5972-21e6-4fe0-a179-f8bba8934ca0", "type": "concept", "title": "Newtonian, shear-thinning, shear-thickening, and time-dependent behavior"}, {"id": "45896794-bf98-486b-8f00-9651d4e4d16a", "type": "concept", "title": "Texture profile concepts, fracture, crispness, chewiness, spreadability, and instrumental–sensory correlation"}]}, {"id": "fda099b0-b5cd-4b76-842b-99d907cefecb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Phase transitions and food structure", "description": "Students learn how temperature, composition, and process history create or destroy structure.", "children": [{"id": "97a66d34-7685-4c11-b697-9ad1e127708a", "type": "concept", "title": "Melting, crystallization, freezing, evaporation, gelation, glass transition, and collapse"}, {"id": "7d2f8d16-393f-4902-a0c1-07fedee294a4", "type": "concept", "title": "State diagrams and qualitative phase behavior"}, {"id": "edd18f0d-4f7b-4e47-a838-53fc8ed511eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Crystallization control, freeze concentration, staling, caking, stickiness, and structural stability"}]}]}, {"id": "97089312-03d4-4e9e-ad21-e326bfa9cc54", "type": "topic", "title": "Ingredients, Additives, and Formulation", "description": "Ingredients and additives are tools used to achieve safety, stability, sensory quality, nutrition, and manufacturability. Students learn function, interactions, lawful use, and transparent evaluation.", "children": [{"id": "9d802b71-231d-4425-bfed-bde24ba709ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Functional ingredient systems", "description": "Students connect ingredient selection to a defined product requirement.", "children": [{"id": "c567c592-2d80-49b3-9970-81a8a3b05fdb", "type": "concept", "title": "Sweeteners, salts, acids/bases, buffers, leavening agents, emulsifiers, stabilizers, thickeners, gelling agents, humectants, and anticaking agents"}, {"id": "7c06eea4-ec34-44dc-bfc5-11ebcbf5b23d", "type": "concept", "title": "Preservatives, antioxidants, sequestrants, colorants, flavors, enzymes, and processing aids"}, {"id": "11f8744b-03e2-4db7-8bd0-5538745016ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Synergy, antagonism, order of addition, hydration, dispersion, and processing conditions"}]}, {"id": "fa7c62bf-5e38-42f6-b407-275679fb6ae1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Formulation calculations", "description": "This subtopic develops quantitative control over recipes and experimental prototypes.", "children": [{"id": "746ffea3-68bb-42ac-b30d-055f346370e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Mass balance, percentages, baker’s percent, concentration, dilution, yield, overrun, and moisture/solids balance"}, {"id": "fb8cec8e-e484-417a-8a91-5a075325673b", "type": "concept", "title": "Ingredient substitution, nutrient targets, allergen constraints, sodium/sugar/fat reduction, and cost"}, {"id": "c7e28736-ed29-4321-aa9a-2a61d5505fc2", "type": "concept", "title": "Unit conversion, tolerances, rounding, scale-up assumptions, and verification"}]}, {"id": "d21b4f59-8062-4c80-8caf-27a1185998ae", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regulatory and evidence considerations", "description": "Students distinguish technological function from marketing claims and evaluate safety within lawful conditions of use.", "children": [{"id": "ee68ed34-4a6e-4a4f-9518-6e8900c8d9ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Food additive versus ingredient versus processing aid definitions by jurisdiction"}, {"id": "0b58953e-31fc-40af-8e2b-44634e9a273c", "type": "concept", "title": "Approved uses, specifications, maximum levels, labeling, allergens, and clean-label tradeoffs"}, {"id": "915f8cb1-47ac-41dc-8cef-2e950b914a5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk assessment concepts: hazard, exposure, acceptable intake, uncertainty, and post-market evidence"}]}]}, {"id": "bf4a6013-ccef-4d0a-8b4b-d3e6c267be20", "type": "topic", "title": "Thermal and Nonthermal Food Processing", "description": "Processing changes safety, shelf life, texture, flavor, color, and nutrients. This section integrates heat and mass transfer with microbial/enzyme inactivation and quality retention.", "children": [{"id": "7a12767d-caa6-4fc9-8501-eec0c4bca3b5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Heat transfer and thermal operations", "description": "Students connect conduction, convection, and radiation to heating rate and process uniformity.", "children": [{"id": "bc6308ce-c086-425f-b30c-514d5c0ebc97", "type": "concept", "title": "Blanching, pasteurization, sterilization, canning/retorting, cooking, baking, frying, roasting, extrusion, evaporation, and aseptic processing"}, {"id": "3026b98b-284f-4a5d-929e-44f22dd7f3c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Heating curves, cold spots, come-up time, heat penetration, and process factors"}, {"id": "0ff1eeba-9503-4be6-95b8-8bd377212d0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Microbial lethality concepts, D/z/F values at an introductory level, and quality kinetics"}]}, {"id": "234d0376-9ea8-4242-910c-bd4fa3cf8c87", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mass transfer and concentration", "description": "This subtopic explains how water and solutes move during drying, osmotic treatment, frying, and concentration.", "children": [{"id": "2af3d358-9450-4141-9fd2-26e06474f2e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Diffusion, gradients, boundary layers, surface area, porosity, and shrinkage"}, {"id": "06f25b72-746d-4269-a71e-b6c8514df973", "type": "concept", "title": "Drying methods, evaporation, membrane concentration, osmotic dehydration, and infusion"}, {"id": "cfe0ee2b-63e6-4ffe-8911-e418a947f2c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Case hardening, rehydration, volatile loss, nutrient retention, and energy use"}]}, {"id": "27c2f60f-a1fd-4c5f-a323-77c301496c7b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nonthermal and emerging technologies", "description": "Students compare alternatives using mechanisms, evidence, scale, regulation, and product fit.", "children": [{"id": "69632761-3ebf-4394-b0a4-a74e32007454", "type": "concept", "title": "High-pressure processing, pulsed electric fields, cold plasma, UV, irradiation, ultrasound, ohmic heating, and microwave/radio-frequency applications"}, {"id": "91e6183f-e716-4ec7-a7df-f1ba39884caf", "type": "concept", "title": "Mechanisms of microbial/enzyme control and quality change"}, {"id": "ad806825-18d5-433f-8b25-589b56c30935", "type": "concept", "title": "Validation, equipment limits, packaging compatibility, consumer perception, cost, and sustainability tradeoffs"}]}]}, {"id": "60e2137c-9e1e-4f7c-b4bd-727282040ad5", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Microbiology, Fermentation, and Safety", "description": "Microorganisms can spoil food, cause disease, or create valued products. Students learn ecological principles, control strategies, and beneficial fermentation relevant to nutrition and product quality.", "children": [{"id": "972276a7-4f3b-40f4-b5d8-ebedbb621499", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Microbial ecology of foods", "description": "Students connect organism needs to food composition, environment, processing, and storage.", "children": [{"id": "caa4fd82-bca3-4ecd-98c9-4b9de3beca95", "type": "concept", "title": "Bacteria, yeasts, molds, viruses, parasites, spores, biofilms, and bacteriophages"}, {"id": "8c053a9a-83a3-4618-bd41-fa2a48791eca", "type": "concept", "title": "Intrinsic factors: pH, water activity, nutrients, structure, redox, and antimicrobials"}, {"id": "0515967b-3d7a-42e7-b4e7-c67e769f5231", "type": "concept", "title": "Extrinsic/implicit factors: temperature, atmosphere, humidity, competition, and packaging"}]}, {"id": "6a280733-4066-44a0-9832-775cc6869dc5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Spoilage, pathogens, and control", "description": "This subtopic distinguishes safety from quality and emphasizes multiple hurdles.", "children": [{"id": "e9265030-9f69-48ad-8e56-7738f472ba35", "type": "concept", "title": "Spoilage signs, shelf-life failure, foodborne infection/intoxication, and toxin concerns"}, {"id": "2b2c4b27-895c-45f7-ae46-c7f214d89f07", "type": "concept", "title": "Thermal control, refrigeration/freezing, pH, water activity, preservatives, atmosphere, hygiene, and competing cultures"}, {"id": "4dd0fbf3-b3f7-4977-9577-0d3a75762857", "type": "concept", "title": "Hurdle technology, predictive concepts, challenge studies, validation, and risk-based control"}]}, {"id": "ee06b12e-d1d8-42f5-889f-69af075c3fb5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fermentation science", "description": "Students examine how controlled microbial metabolism changes safety, preservation, flavor, texture, and potential health effects.", "children": [{"id": "d7803e01-593d-419a-a4b3-d5e3720a5ccb", "type": "concept", "title": "Lactic, alcoholic, acetic, mold, and alkaline fermentations"}, {"id": "1a774b97-d310-40d6-9335-6a11427c1a57", "type": "concept", "title": "Starter cultures, indigenous fermentation, substrates, kinetics, pH, salt, temperature, oxygen, and endpoint control"}, {"id": "d4892b3f-84b1-4d79-b1e7-f1a606313b87", "type": "concept", "title": "Yogurt, cheese, bread, vegetables, legumes, cocoa/coffee, vinegar, and beverages"}, {"id": "4f13b83a-0c9d-4b69-bf07-2d2a00279aec", "type": "concept", "title": "Probiotics, live cultures, fermented foods, claims, survival, and evidence limitations"}]}]}, {"id": "facb891d-536c-4177-b42c-359ade403e26", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Preservation, Packaging, and Shelf Life", "description": "Shelf life ends when safety, quality, nutrition, or acceptability no longer meets specifications. This section integrates preservation hurdles with packaging and evidence-based shelf-life design.", "children": [{"id": "f923d872-c873-4b3e-8195-725a2200bfdf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Preservation strategies and hurdle design", "description": "Students choose combinations that fit the food, target organisms/reactions, consumer use, and distribution.", "children": [{"id": "3f4825e6-90ba-41fa-94ca-9d6f14030236", "type": "concept", "title": "Temperature control, drying, concentration, acidification, fermentation, preservatives, atmosphere, and thermal/nonthermal processing"}, {"id": "8edd96cb-42f4-472d-9c72-f2a4955ac3cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Synergistic hurdles, process severity, quality tradeoffs, and failure modes"}, {"id": "731323c5-a1a0-4bfa-92b6-b476f1beaf22", "type": "concept", "title": "Home/cultural preservation versus validated commercial processes"}]}, {"id": "00009c8d-721b-4202-aeb6-3ee7da6b12b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Packaging functions and materials", "description": "This subtopic treats packaging as part of the food system, not decoration.", "children": [{"id": "9da9451c-bfd6-45c3-bca1-260418d890e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Containment, protection, communication, convenience, portioning, tamper evidence, and traceability"}, {"id": "20e8de86-7647-4388-ade4-32424441d766", "type": "concept", "title": "Glass, metal, paper/paperboard, polymers, multilayers, coatings, active packaging, and intelligent indicators"}, {"id": "e3b8a1d1-4781-43af-88ed-f2a6fc4c29a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Moisture/oxygen/light/aroma barriers, seal integrity, migration, headspace, and modified atmosphere"}]}, {"id": "5ae8531e-7e5c-4d0f-950f-dc61ec113766", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Shelf-life study design", "description": "Students establish failure criteria and collect data under realistic and accelerated conditions.", "children": [{"id": "a9b2862e-656e-40a2-b64b-e14ca6709f10", "type": "concept", "title": "Safety, microbial, chemical, physical, sensory, nutritional, and package failure endpoints"}, {"id": "79fd22f9-e399-4193-be62-2e168e8dfd38", "type": "concept", "title": "Real-time, accelerated, abuse, opened-package, and distribution studies"}, {"id": "52e46183-3a2b-45cd-b390-b2ff14271180", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling intervals, replication, kinetic models, uncertainty, date marking, and consumer instructions"}]}, {"id": "a9b6a727-4d8f-44dc-9955-e3e731013c7c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Packaging sustainability and food-waste tradeoffs", "description": "Students avoid one-dimensional judgments by considering product loss, material impacts, reuse/recycling systems, and actual infrastructure.", "children": [{"id": "a06a7e4d-9c85-496b-bfda-b786c6b25368", "type": "concept", "title": "Source reduction, lightweighting, reuse, recyclability, compostability, and renewable content"}, {"id": "00f244d5-adb0-4082-9496-45e0eec99730", "type": "concept", "title": "Life-cycle thinking, functional unit, food loss prevention, transport efficiency, and contamination"}, {"id": "445379f9-32f4-4058-9860-ef7433bfd974", "type": "concept", "title": "Design for recovery, labeling claims, local systems, and unintended consequences"}]}]}, {"id": "65ca26fa-8f88-4311-8282-53b14d0e8ce1", "type": "topic", "title": "Sensory Science, Consumer Testing, and Statistics", "description": "Human perception is a scientific measurement domain. Students learn to select methods, control bias, analyze data, and distinguish product differences from consumer liking.", "children": [{"id": "c4f6a14a-29b5-4bf7-9af8-70843f8dd0c0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sensory perception and test environment", "description": "Students connect appearance, aroma, taste, texture, and chemesthesis to physiology and context.", "children": [{"id": "87e863bf-8b1d-47d6-9b57-1712d5f62c8e", "type": "concept", "title": "Orthonasal/retronasal olfaction, taste, trigeminal sensation, vision, hearing, touch, and oral processing"}, {"id": "e8ab99eb-b318-45bb-8752-eaa57801ab75", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptation, fatigue, carryover, expectation, halo, order, contrast, and context effects"}, {"id": "65582173-1ee2-47ce-b20d-dc7f7517adf3", "type": "concept", "title": "Controlled booths/rooms, lighting, temperature, serving order, coding, palate cleansing, and sample handling"}]}, {"id": "ba0545fb-25e7-404a-a58a-f0c3aba3c949", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Analytical sensory methods", "description": "This subtopic measures whether products differ and how their attributes differ.", "children": [{"id": "f33363dd-500c-4d73-a02e-ffd04eff054e", "type": "concept", "title": "Triangle, duo-trio, paired comparison, tetrad, and threshold concepts"}, {"id": "76d9bf5a-688d-4f67-a811-37ab067b3271", "type": "concept", "title": "Descriptive analysis, panel selection/training, lexicons, references, calibration, and repeatability"}, {"id": "0c1b7a88-a748-4c12-9019-bd0f36ed1e60", "type": "concept", "title": "Difference-from-control, ranking, quality grading, and time-intensity concepts"}]}, {"id": "fc6b500a-5a69-4dac-8f08-086bf3d9123f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Affective and consumer methods", "description": "Students learn what liking and preference can—and cannot—tell product developers and dietetics professionals.", "children": [{"id": "0e0ab5f7-d9ba-4a17-a3c5-78b5f3a19241", "type": "concept", "title": "Hedonic scales, preference, just-about-right, purchase intent, check-all-that-apply, and open response"}, {"id": "51543d43-c2fd-4ebd-bbb7-67366b591c34", "type": "concept", "title": "Target population, recruitment, sample size, central-location/home-use tests, and cultural context"}, {"id": "4a76b405-1767-4a45-8d47-dc105207d703", "type": "concept", "title": "Segmentation, penalties, demand characteristics, ethics, privacy, and generalizability"}]}, {"id": "8a59be8f-676b-40fc-89d6-69e667c5c3e0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Experimental design and statistics", "description": "Students use statistics as a design and reasoning tool rather than a final software button.", "children": [{"id": "dd949e60-319c-4976-8d5e-a700d0e2e3ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Randomization, blocking, counterbalancing, replication, independent units, and repeated measures"}, {"id": "7cd3fe08-8ca0-4092-ad21-aca1e59c7710", "type": "concept", "title": "Descriptive statistics, confidence intervals, t tests, chi-square, ANOVA, post-hoc comparisons, correlation, and regression at an introductory level"}, {"id": "c930d9e1-f647-43e9-9dfd-24d62959d12e", "type": "concept", "title": "Effect size, multiple testing, assumptions, missing data, visualization, and practical versus statistical significance"}]}]}, {"id": "77a1fe82-85a4-40ce-9a89-a24d2ad630d3", "type": "topic", "title": "Nutrition, Processing, Quality, Regulation, and Sustainability", "description": "Food science decisions change nutrient composition, bioaccessibility, safety, cost, acceptability, and environmental impact. This section helps dietetics students evaluate processing without simplistic “processed/unprocessed” binaries.", "children": [{"id": "2c5a7c95-9d60-49ec-a791-e1dbd9572bad", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Processing and nutritional quality", "description": "Students analyze mechanisms and tradeoffs rather than assuming all processing is beneficial or harmful.", "children": [{"id": "dc4e6c4e-901c-4b0c-bb1a-d74c5bd0215d", "type": "concept", "title": "Retention, leaching, oxidation, thermal degradation, matrix disruption, particle size, and bioaccessibility"}, {"id": "7050fdf9-1219-4638-a2ea-ee77eb377234", "type": "concept", "title": "Fortification, enrichment, reformulation, fermentation, germination, extrusion, and cooking"}, {"id": "ae9f8c5a-1c17-410b-84a1-900701a43cb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Digestibility, glycemic response, satiety, sodium/sugar/fat reduction, and sensory compensation"}]}, {"id": "6b67c2f8-7d27-4e13-9a95-e03d35a98094", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Quality assurance and food law", "description": "This subtopic connects specifications and process control to truthful, consistent, lawful products.", "children": [{"id": "c37f421d-e310-4c62-9c8f-efee9cb22163", "type": "concept", "title": "Quality attributes, specifications, tolerance, control charts, acceptance sampling, nonconformance, and corrective action"}, {"id": "07a89b9e-31b4-4bf8-a07a-987b55ee1aee", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity standards, ingredients, additives, allergens, nutrition labeling, claims, net quantity, traceability, and recalls"}, {"id": "6397ad43-24ae-448b-9883-ccc633c5314f", "type": "concept", "title": "Regulatory jurisdiction, validated methods, documentation, and change control"}]}, {"id": "9d1bce1f-c73b-4a20-877a-d3cfc359ecf0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sustainable food science", "description": "Students evaluate environmental and social consequences with a systems lens.", "children": [{"id": "fcf44347-8f9e-4d1a-a6cc-a9885a33b040", "type": "concept", "title": "Energy, water, emissions, land, biodiversity, labor, by-products, waste, cold chain, and food loss"}, {"id": "035b4c00-c9d9-4ccb-b4d7-41bd71d0c6d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Life-cycle assessment concepts, functional units, boundaries, allocation, tradeoffs, and uncertainty"}, {"id": "bc66972e-a88b-468f-bcd7-069704b21aa0", "type": "concept", "title": "Upcycling, side-stream valorization, circularity, local/global supply, and nutrition-sensitive sustainability"}]}, {"id": "e87c0db9-f70a-4a38-ad80-b22be2dcb24a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Emerging foods and responsible innovation", "description": "Students assess novel ingredients and technologies using evidence, safety, nutrition, function, equity, regulation, and consumer context.", "children": [{"id": "1d581562-7ee9-490f-9455-5e541a6b0ab2", "type": "concept", "title": "Plant-based, precision-fermented, cultivated-cell, insect, algae, novel-protein, and personalized products"}, {"id": "e998c7ad-987c-4881-b3f6-a5cb618fb9da", "type": "concept", "title": "Ultra-processing debates, classification systems, confounding, reformulation, and health-outcome evidence"}, {"id": "caf705c2-8099-437d-a578-466fff1338a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Responsible claims, access, cultural fit, intellectual property, transparency, and unintended consequences"}]}]}, {"id": "65968e15-0c6c-490b-938e-2e48eb4ad712", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Product Development and Capstone Experiment", "description": "The capstone integrates composition, processing, safety, sensory science, nutrition, quality, cost, and sustainability. Students design an experiment—not merely a recipe trial—and defend decisions with evidence.", "children": [{"id": "e13552ca-bc7f-45d1-9c4c-bbd4d6d0cc31", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Problem definition and product brief", "description": "Students identify a real user need and translate it into measurable design requirements.", "children": [{"id": "85cb10e2-5304-41b9-b926-6210f4b4e12b", "type": "concept", "title": "Target user, eating occasion, need state, competitive set, value proposition, and constraints"}, {"id": "24e5c91e-b68b-4392-adfd-35bd2dbd61c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Nutrition, sensory, safety, shelf-life, preparation, packaging, cost, accessibility, and sustainability targets"}, {"id": "59b70e69-e007-4831-bfda-e4a2dc094cc5", "type": "concept", "title": "Literature and patent search, benchmark products, assumptions, and risk register"}]}, {"id": "34f099b0-b5e6-4752-b8f5-d40ef5e743f3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Prototype and experimental design", "description": "This subtopic turns formulation variables into an efficient, interpretable test plan.", "children": [{"id": "79de51be-825e-47b2-987e-096ecf5e7470", "type": "concept", "title": "Control and treatment formulations, factors, levels, responses, and acceptance criteria"}, {"id": "aaa8d473-f77b-4db5-949c-dd2d070b7df9", "type": "concept", "title": "Mass balance, scaling, standardized procedure, process parameters, randomization, replication, and blocking"}, {"id": "f3af0a02-6fe6-43b1-8598-a5e5ef6d05fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Pilot trials, screening, factorial concepts, mixture-design awareness, and iteration"}]}, {"id": "0c3e2d03-dc12-4220-ae10-54586144296f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Measurement and evidence integration", "description": "Students combine instrumental, sensory, nutritional, safety, and process evidence without overstating conclusions.", "children": [{"id": "689c7e35-1164-47be-9580-3723ce61343a", "type": "concept", "title": "Yield, moisture/water activity, pH, color, texture/viscosity, stability, nutrient calculation, and other fit-for-purpose measures"}, {"id": "ec098e00-47fe-4050-a973-9b7092e659be", "type": "concept", "title": "Sensory method selection, consumer limitations, statistical analysis, graphs, uncertainty, and discrepancies"}, {"id": "431a63f7-1967-45a8-beae-5b5b5bf47a7b", "type": "concept", "title": "Ingredient functionality, mechanism, tradeoffs, and decision matrix"}]}, {"id": "78548c0f-715e-40a5-8567-bc29707301c2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technical communication and reproducibility", "description": "Students present a product and scientific argument that another team could audit and repeat.", "children": [{"id": "168d53af-787c-43f6-8ed5-67a31bbc8329", "type": "concept", "title": "Formula, process flow, specifications, critical parameters, equipment, records, and version history"}, {"id": "fb98baef-c3a2-41c8-8232-1c18987c26f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Results, discussion, limitations, cost/nutrition estimate, safety and regulatory considerations, packaging, and shelf-life plan"}, {"id": "a61341fe-144c-4341-9f9f-8dd1e16dc7b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Poster, technical report, oral defense, sample presentation, peer review, and next-step recommendations"}]}]}], "dbBacked": true}$fscijson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'food-science'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Food Science',
  goal = 'Food Science',
  summary = 'This course examines how food composition, molecular structure, physical properties, microorganisms, processing, packaging, and storage determine safety, nutritional quality, sensory properties, functionality, and shelf life. Students study water, carbohydrates, proteins, enzymes, lipids, micronutrients, pigments, flavors, colloids, rheology, additives, thermal and nonthermal processing, fermentation, preservation, packaging, sensory science, quality assurance, regulation, and sustainability. Laboratory work emphasizes safe technique, standardized preparation, analytical measurement, experimental design, statistics, scientific writing, and an evidence-based product-development project relevant to nutrition and dietetics.',
  data = $fscijson${"slug": "food-science", "title": "Food Science", "description": "This course examines how food composition, molecular structure, physical properties, microorganisms, processing, packaging, and storage determine safety, nutritional quality, sensory properties, functionality, and shelf life. Students study water, carbohydrates, proteins, enzymes, lipids, micronutrients, pigments, flavors, colloids, rheology, additives, thermal and nonthermal processing, fermentation, preservation, packaging, sensory science, quality assurance, regulation, and sustainability. Laboratory work emphasizes safe technique, standardized preparation, analytical measurement, experimental design, statistics, scientific writing, and an evidence-based product-development project relevant to nutrition and dietetics.", "topics": [{"id": "f756f463-c99b-40e2-b218-b461961fabc6", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Science Foundations and Experimental Thinking", "description": "This opening section defines food science as an interdisciplinary study of composition, structure, processing, safety, quality, nutrition, and consumer experience. Students learn to ask testable questions and connect molecular change to measurable food behavior.", "children": [{"id": "f20c6c79-6df9-41c5-9f49-3c6cf96e78a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Food as a complex system", "description": "Students move beyond ingredient lists to see foods as dynamic biological and physical systems.", "children": [{"id": "989f0e6d-7be2-41c8-8e96-e11b01015ca2", "type": "concept", "title": "Food matrices, phases, interfaces, structure, and hierarchy"}, {"id": "969b43fd-9341-48c2-9750-3ff8561f54cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Composition–structure–process–property relationships"}, {"id": "e027e743-3bfb-404c-b0d5-4f52345f8a73", "type": "concept", "title": "Raw materials, formulation, processing, storage, preparation, digestion, and consumption"}]}, {"id": "d4f70548-eb61-4cea-89a9-39f23b592913", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scientific method and evidence", "description": "This subtopic establishes how food scientists design comparisons, control variation, and make claims supported by data.", "children": [{"id": "4a366049-0a46-4fe8-b107-1109494aace1", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question, hypothesis, mechanism, prediction, and operational definition"}, {"id": "11892c05-8427-431c-ad59-fee1fe7686db", "type": "concept", "title": "Independent, dependent, controlled, confounding, and nuisance variables"}, {"id": "dd487de3-60ea-4009-a4dc-304a63836795", "type": "concept", "title": "Replication, randomization, controls, blinding, uncertainty, reproducibility, and causal inference"}]}, {"id": "563c422d-7e78-4134-9e21-3e977d7487de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Laboratory safety and scientific integrity", "description": "Students learn to work safely and document methods and data so another person can understand and repeat the work.", "children": [{"id": "e177dc12-92c4-4701-b2a1-203802e1eb70", "type": "concept", "title": "Chemical, thermal, electrical, mechanical, pressure, sharp, allergen, and biological hazards"}, {"id": "9258a695-40de-49ff-8c03-0d77a817f3d0", "type": "concept", "title": "PPE, safety data sheets, risk assessment, waste, equipment training, and emergency response"}, {"id": "faea5edd-4fb3-4541-bbe6-f06cc432521b", "type": "concept", "title": "Laboratory notebook, raw data, units, significant figures, version control, attribution, and no fabrication"}]}]}, {"id": "a87cafb1-8825-4bf4-9683-c0f65bdabc5d", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Composition and Analytical Measurement", "description": "Composition data underpins nutrition, labeling, formulation, quality, and research. This section teaches students what common measurements mean, how they are obtained, and where error enters.", "children": [{"id": "e8704953-e67a-4c08-9add-8e307a7d6b48", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Proximate and component analysis", "description": "Students learn the logic and limitations of measuring moisture, ash, protein, lipid, carbohydrate, fiber, and energy.", "children": [{"id": "13da0ee7-ddba-48fa-ab95-04f29650cea1", "type": "concept", "title": "Direct measurement versus calculation by difference"}, {"id": "f1a66cfd-8dfb-4540-886e-aab88988e73e", "type": "concept", "title": "Kjeldahl/Dumas concepts, solvent extraction, gravimetry, ashing, and dietary-fiber methods"}, {"id": "b1fbac74-d948-4b7e-b6a1-5801a35c3591", "type": "concept", "title": "Wet basis, dry basis, conversion factors, recovery, and matrix effects"}]}, {"id": "c5e0a228-5ae0-4e38-8324-4439789a7cc7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sampling and sample preparation", "description": "This subtopic explains why a precise instrument cannot rescue an unrepresentative sample.", "children": [{"id": "ad5aaf00-3846-4876-855a-90fc96d91559", "type": "concept", "title": "Population, lot, increment, composite, laboratory sample, and analytical portion"}, {"id": "d32a9a1f-086e-4cb1-b16f-e841f351e043", "type": "concept", "title": "Homogenization, grinding, subsampling, storage, preservation, and contamination prevention"}, {"id": "ccc5f7b2-3c84-4b78-82dd-2ef2f9624264", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling error, heterogeneity, stratification, and chain of custody"}]}, {"id": "4d471b3f-d2dc-4a13-a7aa-57502b2dba96", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Method quality and data interpretation", "description": "Students evaluate whether a method is fit for purpose and whether a difference is meaningful.", "children": [{"id": "fd677266-5478-45bb-89ae-30b06df80d4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Accuracy, precision, bias, sensitivity, selectivity, detection/quantitation limits, range, and robustness"}, {"id": "7f352046-e70a-4284-a6c0-2224b4d4cd48", "type": "concept", "title": "Calibration, blanks, standards, controls, spikes, duplicates, reference materials, and uncertainty"}, {"id": "c433e9ca-9f30-4f55-ace7-4e3ef8a76e4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Validated methods, quality-control charts, outliers, significant figures, and comparison with FoodData Central"}]}]}, {"id": "d791e86e-b525-4f16-b4eb-d19698d9901d", "type": "topic", "title": "Water, Moisture, and Water Activity", "description": "Water controls texture, mobility, reactions, microbial growth, freezing, and shelf life. Students learn why total moisture and available water are not the same concept.", "children": [{"id": "e9ff696f-e263-4963-bc13-2c3c0ed3ec5e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Molecular properties and states of water", "description": "Students connect hydrogen bonding and molecular mobility to food structure and processing behavior.", "children": [{"id": "f90ae109-4919-4329-91b6-a3581dbe8e91", "type": "concept", "title": "Free, bound, immobilized, and structural water"}, {"id": "9b9c0297-e58f-4aac-b787-c15a6716a54e", "type": "concept", "title": "Water as solvent, plasticizer, reactant, and heat-transfer medium"}, {"id": "7f498bbd-a4ad-4204-aa86-248a5ab7388a", "type": "concept", "title": "Ice formation, supercooling, glass transition, freeze concentration, and thawing"}]}, {"id": "b3832a14-b03c-4d28-bf25-798376589cf8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Moisture content versus water activity", "description": "This subtopic distinguishes the quantity of water from its thermodynamic availability.", "children": [{"id": "58d473d0-bbed-4e0e-bf8b-ec62c482b623", "type": "concept", "title": "Water activity definition and measurement concepts"}, {"id": "70eb16e5-af42-4bfd-8806-35cf65620a4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Microbial growth limits, reaction rates, texture, and ingredient interactions"}, {"id": "a14a5119-a4ce-4132-a6f4-afff1c705b8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Humectants, salt/sugar effects, temperature effects, and equilibrium relative humidity"}]}, {"id": "1901c258-fccc-41cc-ba9a-0bc1c1f76c9e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sorption and moisture control", "description": "Students use sorption behavior to reason about drying, packaging, caking, crispness, and stability.", "children": [{"id": "3d463118-7448-4105-81f5-20277035684e", "type": "concept", "title": "Adsorption/desorption isotherms and hysteresis"}, {"id": "a40737c2-9c4d-473e-a72e-4d1c4fabecd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Monolayer region, capillary condensation, and critical moisture"}, {"id": "40fc8e51-16cd-4a57-956c-8bf0264031d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Drying, concentration, moisture migration, barrier packaging, and shelf-life prediction"}]}]}, {"id": "62c4d059-f66f-4847-b581-f7ef3a9a812d", "type": "topic", "title": "Carbohydrates in Foods", "description": "Carbohydrates provide sweetness, bulk, structure, viscosity, gelation, browning, and dietary fiber. This section links molecular structure to culinary, industrial, and nutritional function.", "children": [{"id": "9df47153-5e71-45fe-bea3-2876d0494c01", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sugars and small carbohydrates", "description": "Students examine how structure affects sweetness, solubility, crystallization, water binding, and chemical reactivity.", "children": [{"id": "dac7fe0b-697b-4584-b3e3-cf19dda4a33e", "type": "concept", "title": "Monosaccharides, disaccharides, oligosaccharides, reducing sugars, and sugar alcohols"}, {"id": "e2f64e71-d298-4786-ad10-3491db513627", "type": "concept", "title": "Sweetness, hygroscopicity, humectancy, colligative effects, inversion, and crystallization"}, {"id": "a9a62d7e-26a3-4dd4-8368-11dc2bccd0d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Caramelization and Maillard participation"}]}, {"id": "42cd1290-389a-4e37-a9a0-af3afb81f6f5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Starch and hydrocolloids", "description": "This subtopic explains thickening, gelation, stability, and texture across sauces, baked goods, and processed foods.", "children": [{"id": "bc1c6d27-edd6-406c-85d1-643f3de99220", "type": "concept", "title": "Amylose, amylopectin, granules, gelatinization, pasting, gelation, retrogradation, and syneresis"}, {"id": "fb16530a-81ab-47f7-998d-000ab2a98407", "type": "concept", "title": "Modified starches, gums, pectins, cellulose derivatives, alginates, and carrageenans"}, {"id": "499dc0f3-0b82-48c4-8948-c16221771fc0", "type": "concept", "title": "Viscosity, suspension, water binding, freeze–thaw stability, and process tolerance"}]}, {"id": "2a7a98f5-cecd-4c68-ac36-373f2069e91f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dietary fiber and carbohydrate quality", "description": "Students connect physicochemical properties to digestion, glycemic response, fermentation, satiety, and formulation.", "children": [{"id": "f724907f-f8ea-478c-9b8a-df963b8461f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Soluble/insoluble, viscous/nonviscous, fermentable/nonfermentable classifications"}, {"id": "5428cb9e-58ac-44a6-b5a6-413f03e05625", "type": "concept", "title": "Resistant starch, beta-glucans, inulin/fructans, pectins, and cell-wall matrices"}, {"id": "88ff35b9-b6fa-4c08-bec5-5c62b90a3426", "type": "concept", "title": "Processing effects, particle size, food structure, analytical definitions, and nutrition claims"}]}]}, {"id": "0fb71ef8-a291-4a54-8873-97062ee407f8", "type": "topic", "title": "Proteins, Enzymes, and Protein Functionality", "description": "Proteins determine structure, water binding, foaming, emulsification, gelation, and nutrient quality. Enzymes drive desirable and undesirable transformations during processing and storage.", "children": [{"id": "99d0b1e8-8fef-490e-ba94-e39afec5b958", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Protein structure and stability", "description": "Students connect amino-acid sequence and molecular forces to folding, denaturation, aggregation, and digestibility.", "children": [{"id": "8bc6fcf9-d904-423c-9f5b-624cdb9f4443", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary through quaternary structure; globular, fibrous, and intrinsically disordered regions"}, {"id": "b3a34b74-14a6-4378-a8d8-1fdc64060f3d", "type": "concept", "title": "Hydrogen bonding, hydrophobic interactions, ionic forces, and disulfide bonds"}, {"id": "2f68ba21-b484-49eb-9ee1-8305aebad23d", "type": "concept", "title": "Heat, pH, salts, shear, solvents, oxidation, and pressure effects"}]}, {"id": "8e279286-4802-4580-bfac-1645ec24cb04", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Functional properties in foods", "description": "This subtopic explains how proteins create texture and stabilize air, water, and fat phases.", "children": [{"id": "b7d6f3f8-66c7-46f2-b787-3aad53f880ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Solubility, hydration, water-holding, swelling, viscosity, gelation, adhesion, and film formation"}, {"id": "236b51eb-332a-499d-a9ad-8c6564bf8112", "type": "concept", "title": "Foams and emulsions: adsorption, interfacial films, whipping, overrun, drainage, and coalescence"}, {"id": "48d26986-3afc-4fa7-b1ab-226f8090c945", "type": "concept", "title": "Gluten networks, egg coagulation, milk proteins, meat proteins, legumes, and plant-protein isolates"}]}, {"id": "95f92d9e-a6a2-442e-95c7-3793a9225c74", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Enzymes in preparation and processing", "description": "Students learn how enzymes can soften, brown, clarify, tenderize, coagulate, generate flavor, or destroy quality.", "children": [{"id": "94708de2-e026-452e-9e59-7d9fbd5c519e", "type": "concept", "title": "Catalysis, specificity, cofactors, rate, temperature, pH, inhibition, and inactivation"}, {"id": "db629b69-3df4-4148-87ed-292a8430bc51", "type": "concept", "title": "Polyphenol oxidase, pectinases, amylases, proteases, lipases, rennet, transglutaminase, and lactase"}, {"id": "bb309734-451d-4962-90aa-a47c7eb200a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Blanching, enzyme assays, immobilization concepts, and processing applications"}]}, {"id": "90732290-4baf-4967-8ed3-0f4c45090f5e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nutritional quality of proteins", "description": "Students relate food processing and matrix effects to amino-acid availability and protein quality.", "children": [{"id": "860743ec-337c-4613-b031-d11e9ae9d5c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Essential amino acids, limiting amino acid, digestibility, complementarity, and quality scores"}, {"id": "a8eebead-1166-4244-80e8-f4275ace8796", "type": "concept", "title": "Denaturation versus destruction; antinutrients and digestibility"}, {"id": "39e88482-ab46-490f-9485-6c8c21b49b83", "type": "concept", "title": "Maillard-related lysine loss, oxidation, heat damage, and processing tradeoffs"}]}]}, {"id": "5c4b97a9-d836-4c21-8144-880600f32efa", "type": "topic", "title": "Lipids in Foods", "description": "Lipids contribute energy, essential fatty acids, flavor, mouthfeel, structure, heat transfer, and bioactive delivery. This section connects composition and physical state to functionality and stability.", "children": [{"id": "af28e7e0-be28-472d-b3ff-6dde8ca2bb22", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Lipid classes and molecular structure", "description": "Students distinguish major lipid types and relate saturation and chain structure to properties.", "children": [{"id": "46df7bd1-ee2a-4d9f-8681-ad07ae652fa9", "type": "concept", "title": "Fatty acids, triacylglycerols, phospholipids, sterols, waxes, and fat-soluble compounds"}, {"id": "3283ffdb-eddb-482c-9d82-14f7db24c908", "type": "concept", "title": "Saturation, cis/trans configuration, chain length, melting, and polarity"}, {"id": "135756c3-8dba-41c4-a772-955486832cb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Essential fatty acids and nutritional context"}]}, {"id": "be8e07fa-a046-4a8e-a245-8ed8d557a0a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fat crystallization and functionality", "description": "This subtopic explains why fats can be plastic, spreadable, brittle, smooth, or grainy.", "children": [{"id": "3c7e9d2e-4627-41a2-8b2a-f2f8be8e5fcd", "type": "concept", "title": "Nucleation, crystal growth, polymorphism, solid-fat content, and tempering"}, {"id": "9ad2d820-b0d2-4761-9562-4f2767ee9ce1", "type": "concept", "title": "Shortening, aeration, creaming, lamination, chocolate snap/gloss, and fat bloom"}, {"id": "21a7deb1-8561-494c-a234-0aeb802708cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Interesterification, fractionation, blending, and replacement strategies"}]}, {"id": "67ee26f7-5f7f-434a-b351-2d432235c4cf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Emulsions and lipid oxidation", "description": "Students learn to stabilize oil/water systems and prevent rancidity.", "children": [{"id": "445be878-0be0-4184-beca-006d436ab4c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Oil-in-water, water-in-oil, multiple emulsions, emulsifiers, proteins, particles, and homogenization"}, {"id": "65ff7db5-1255-4280-b427-fd32ca78f343", "type": "concept", "title": "Creaming, sedimentation, flocculation, coalescence, Ostwald ripening, and phase inversion"}, {"id": "de4d91d1-4bac-4ff2-9607-83be3855ab50", "type": "concept", "title": "Autoxidation, photooxidation, hydrolysis, prooxidants, antioxidants, packaging, and sensory/nutritional consequences"}]}]}, {"id": "56efe8dd-233e-47a8-94de-dd9a2030b0e1", "type": "topic", "title": "Micronutrients, Pigments, Flavor, and Bioactive Compounds", "description": "Food quality and nutritional value depend on compounds present in small amounts. Students learn how processing, light, oxygen, pH, and matrix interactions change vitamins, minerals, color, aroma, taste, and bioactives.", "children": [{"id": "f01f4106-a76d-4b37-8ec3-7dcd1fa589b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Vitamins and minerals", "description": "Students evaluate stability, bioaccessibility, fortification, and analytical interpretation rather than assuming processing always lowers nutrition.", "children": [{"id": "382076a2-cc4e-48ed-a60d-6af51896e59c", "type": "concept", "title": "Water- and fat-soluble vitamin stability to heat, light, oxygen, pH, and leaching"}, {"id": "ee72f96b-a355-442d-997b-25ebdbdcac6a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mineral stability, oxidation state, solubility, interactions, and processing contamination"}, {"id": "ac4c7638-3d09-43b1-9c72-0300d9748003", "type": "concept", "title": "Fortification, enrichment, overages, premixes, uniformity, and bioavailability"}]}, {"id": "311aeed8-4866-4097-8809-ee5b9a2b8fbb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Natural pigments and color chemistry", "description": "This subtopic connects molecular structure and environment to visual quality.", "children": [{"id": "bd051eab-c963-47a9-ba09-18df9077767d", "type": "concept", "title": "Chlorophylls, carotenoids, anthocyanins, betalains, myoglobin, and melanins"}, {"id": "586650a3-4aa5-4e5d-8c77-3217de15d0dd", "type": "concept", "title": "pH, heat, oxygen, metals, enzymes, light, and packaging effects"}, {"id": "26cf9831-45c6-49fc-83ec-ceee4613977e", "type": "concept", "title": "Color measurement: CIELAB concepts, instruments, standards, and consumer perception"}]}, {"id": "90345954-a15e-463b-8702-628c74f1cb04", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Flavor chemistry and perception", "description": "Students study how volatile and nonvolatile compounds create taste, aroma, chemesthesis, and aftertaste.", "children": [{"id": "de68242f-31aa-446d-8d00-4814fa9fcfb9", "type": "concept", "title": "Basic tastes, aroma, retronasal olfaction, trigeminal sensations, and texture–flavor interactions"}, {"id": "c0fdc59a-2ae9-429a-a52b-47633a5f3212", "type": "concept", "title": "Flavor precursors and formation through Maillard chemistry, caramelization, fermentation, oxidation, and enzymatic reactions"}, {"id": "503e4697-eba6-46ac-b17a-b5f7f1996279", "type": "concept", "title": "Volatility, partitioning, release, binding, matrix effects, off-flavors, and flavor encapsulation"}]}, {"id": "8356c4e2-2a84-47c1-9aa6-4407abecf9c8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bioactives and antinutritional factors", "description": "Students critically assess claims about phytochemicals and processing effects.", "children": [{"id": "5dd06816-32ec-4d1e-aba7-013a25120d98", "type": "concept", "title": "Polyphenols, carotenoids, glucosinolates, phytosterols, and other candidate bioactives"}, {"id": "eacc6d45-dc12-4c6e-8479-08b0745eed59", "type": "concept", "title": "Phytate, oxalate, tannins, lectins, protease inhibitors, and cyanogenic compounds"}, {"id": "cd5e12e6-5a82-464a-a795-21c0ec533f9d", "type": "concept", "title": "Dose, matrix, bioaccessibility, metabolism, evidence quality, and safe processing"}]}]}, {"id": "55ebec3b-c5fa-40f7-99e0-174043fac969", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Physical Chemistry, Colloids, Texture, and Rheology", "description": "Many food qualities emerge from structure across scales rather than composition alone. This section teaches students to reason about dispersions, flow, deformation, and transitions.", "children": [{"id": "025a723f-98ba-4e8f-9a69-c8cbe813950b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Food dispersions and colloids", "description": "Students classify multiphase systems and predict instability mechanisms.", "children": [{"id": "e76e3b0d-d21e-444e-bfb8-7d8985662dff", "type": "concept", "title": "Solutions, suspensions, emulsions, foams, gels, sols, and complex multiphase foods"}, {"id": "344680a1-bcb3-4d8a-8c26-066bf9b2d937", "type": "concept", "title": "Particle/droplet size, surface area, interfacial tension, charge, steric stabilization, and density"}, {"id": "d82370ff-14e0-4f86-8d9b-ea9768fbb831", "type": "concept", "title": "Aggregation, sedimentation, creaming, coalescence, drainage, and phase separation"}]}, {"id": "612b4d8e-0691-48bb-b55a-157313f51c2f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rheology and texture", "description": "This subtopic links force and time to flow and deformation in liquids, semi-solids, and solids.", "children": [{"id": "cc764c46-8d46-4eb7-a686-e4c2896f8894", "type": "concept", "title": "Stress, strain, viscosity, elasticity, viscoelasticity, yield stress, and thixotropy"}, {"id": "7e0f5972-21e6-4fe0-a179-f8bba8934ca0", "type": "concept", "title": "Newtonian, shear-thinning, shear-thickening, and time-dependent behavior"}, {"id": "45896794-bf98-486b-8f00-9651d4e4d16a", "type": "concept", "title": "Texture profile concepts, fracture, crispness, chewiness, spreadability, and instrumental–sensory correlation"}]}, {"id": "fda099b0-b5cd-4b76-842b-99d907cefecb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Phase transitions and food structure", "description": "Students learn how temperature, composition, and process history create or destroy structure.", "children": [{"id": "97a66d34-7685-4c11-b697-9ad1e127708a", "type": "concept", "title": "Melting, crystallization, freezing, evaporation, gelation, glass transition, and collapse"}, {"id": "7d2f8d16-393f-4902-a0c1-07fedee294a4", "type": "concept", "title": "State diagrams and qualitative phase behavior"}, {"id": "edd18f0d-4f7b-4e47-a838-53fc8ed511eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Crystallization control, freeze concentration, staling, caking, stickiness, and structural stability"}]}]}, {"id": "97089312-03d4-4e9e-ad21-e326bfa9cc54", "type": "topic", "title": "Ingredients, Additives, and Formulation", "description": "Ingredients and additives are tools used to achieve safety, stability, sensory quality, nutrition, and manufacturability. Students learn function, interactions, lawful use, and transparent evaluation.", "children": [{"id": "9d802b71-231d-4425-bfed-bde24ba709ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Functional ingredient systems", "description": "Students connect ingredient selection to a defined product requirement.", "children": [{"id": "c567c592-2d80-49b3-9970-81a8a3b05fdb", "type": "concept", "title": "Sweeteners, salts, acids/bases, buffers, leavening agents, emulsifiers, stabilizers, thickeners, gelling agents, humectants, and anticaking agents"}, {"id": "7c06eea4-ec34-44dc-bfc5-11ebcbf5b23d", "type": "concept", "title": "Preservatives, antioxidants, sequestrants, colorants, flavors, enzymes, and processing aids"}, {"id": "11f8744b-03e2-4db7-8bd0-5538745016ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Synergy, antagonism, order of addition, hydration, dispersion, and processing conditions"}]}, {"id": "fa7c62bf-5e38-42f6-b407-275679fb6ae1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Formulation calculations", "description": "This subtopic develops quantitative control over recipes and experimental prototypes.", "children": [{"id": "746ffea3-68bb-42ac-b30d-055f346370e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Mass balance, percentages, baker’s percent, concentration, dilution, yield, overrun, and moisture/solids balance"}, {"id": "fb8cec8e-e484-417a-8a91-5a075325673b", "type": "concept", "title": "Ingredient substitution, nutrient targets, allergen constraints, sodium/sugar/fat reduction, and cost"}, {"id": "c7e28736-ed29-4321-aa9a-2a61d5505fc2", "type": "concept", "title": "Unit conversion, tolerances, rounding, scale-up assumptions, and verification"}]}, {"id": "d21b4f59-8062-4c80-8caf-27a1185998ae", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regulatory and evidence considerations", "description": "Students distinguish technological function from marketing claims and evaluate safety within lawful conditions of use.", "children": [{"id": "ee68ed34-4a6e-4a4f-9518-6e8900c8d9ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Food additive versus ingredient versus processing aid definitions by jurisdiction"}, {"id": "0b58953e-31fc-40af-8e2b-44634e9a273c", "type": "concept", "title": "Approved uses, specifications, maximum levels, labeling, allergens, and clean-label tradeoffs"}, {"id": "915f8cb1-47ac-41dc-8cef-2e950b914a5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk assessment concepts: hazard, exposure, acceptable intake, uncertainty, and post-market evidence"}]}]}, {"id": "bf4a6013-ccef-4d0a-8b4b-d3e6c267be20", "type": "topic", "title": "Thermal and Nonthermal Food Processing", "description": "Processing changes safety, shelf life, texture, flavor, color, and nutrients. This section integrates heat and mass transfer with microbial/enzyme inactivation and quality retention.", "children": [{"id": "7a12767d-caa6-4fc9-8501-eec0c4bca3b5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Heat transfer and thermal operations", "description": "Students connect conduction, convection, and radiation to heating rate and process uniformity.", "children": [{"id": "bc6308ce-c086-425f-b30c-514d5c0ebc97", "type": "concept", "title": "Blanching, pasteurization, sterilization, canning/retorting, cooking, baking, frying, roasting, extrusion, evaporation, and aseptic processing"}, {"id": "3026b98b-284f-4a5d-929e-44f22dd7f3c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Heating curves, cold spots, come-up time, heat penetration, and process factors"}, {"id": "0ff1eeba-9503-4be6-95b8-8bd377212d0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Microbial lethality concepts, D/z/F values at an introductory level, and quality kinetics"}]}, {"id": "234d0376-9ea8-4242-910c-bd4fa3cf8c87", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mass transfer and concentration", "description": "This subtopic explains how water and solutes move during drying, osmotic treatment, frying, and concentration.", "children": [{"id": "2af3d358-9450-4141-9fd2-26e06474f2e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Diffusion, gradients, boundary layers, surface area, porosity, and shrinkage"}, {"id": "06f25b72-746d-4269-a71e-b6c8514df973", "type": "concept", "title": "Drying methods, evaporation, membrane concentration, osmotic dehydration, and infusion"}, {"id": "cfe0ee2b-63e6-4ffe-8911-e418a947f2c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Case hardening, rehydration, volatile loss, nutrient retention, and energy use"}]}, {"id": "27c2f60f-a1fd-4c5f-a323-77c301496c7b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nonthermal and emerging technologies", "description": "Students compare alternatives using mechanisms, evidence, scale, regulation, and product fit.", "children": [{"id": "69632761-3ebf-4394-b0a4-a74e32007454", "type": "concept", "title": "High-pressure processing, pulsed electric fields, cold plasma, UV, irradiation, ultrasound, ohmic heating, and microwave/radio-frequency applications"}, {"id": "91e6183f-e716-4ec7-a7df-f1ba39884caf", "type": "concept", "title": "Mechanisms of microbial/enzyme control and quality change"}, {"id": "ad806825-18d5-433f-8b25-589b56c30935", "type": "concept", "title": "Validation, equipment limits, packaging compatibility, consumer perception, cost, and sustainability tradeoffs"}]}]}, {"id": "60e2137c-9e1e-4f7c-b4bd-727282040ad5", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Microbiology, Fermentation, and Safety", "description": "Microorganisms can spoil food, cause disease, or create valued products. Students learn ecological principles, control strategies, and beneficial fermentation relevant to nutrition and product quality.", "children": [{"id": "972276a7-4f3b-40f4-b5d8-ebedbb621499", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Microbial ecology of foods", "description": "Students connect organism needs to food composition, environment, processing, and storage.", "children": [{"id": "caa4fd82-bca3-4ecd-98c9-4b9de3beca95", "type": "concept", "title": "Bacteria, yeasts, molds, viruses, parasites, spores, biofilms, and bacteriophages"}, {"id": "8c053a9a-83a3-4618-bd41-fa2a48791eca", "type": "concept", "title": "Intrinsic factors: pH, water activity, nutrients, structure, redox, and antimicrobials"}, {"id": "0515967b-3d7a-42e7-b4e7-c67e769f5231", "type": "concept", "title": "Extrinsic/implicit factors: temperature, atmosphere, humidity, competition, and packaging"}]}, {"id": "6a280733-4066-44a0-9832-775cc6869dc5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Spoilage, pathogens, and control", "description": "This subtopic distinguishes safety from quality and emphasizes multiple hurdles.", "children": [{"id": "e9265030-9f69-48ad-8e56-7738f472ba35", "type": "concept", "title": "Spoilage signs, shelf-life failure, foodborne infection/intoxication, and toxin concerns"}, {"id": "2b2c4b27-895c-45f7-ae46-c7f214d89f07", "type": "concept", "title": "Thermal control, refrigeration/freezing, pH, water activity, preservatives, atmosphere, hygiene, and competing cultures"}, {"id": "4dd0fbf3-b3f7-4977-9577-0d3a75762857", "type": "concept", "title": "Hurdle technology, predictive concepts, challenge studies, validation, and risk-based control"}]}, {"id": "ee06b12e-d1d8-42f5-889f-69af075c3fb5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fermentation science", "description": "Students examine how controlled microbial metabolism changes safety, preservation, flavor, texture, and potential health effects.", "children": [{"id": "d7803e01-593d-419a-a4b3-d5e3720a5ccb", "type": "concept", "title": "Lactic, alcoholic, acetic, mold, and alkaline fermentations"}, {"id": "1a774b97-d310-40d6-9335-6a11427c1a57", "type": "concept", "title": "Starter cultures, indigenous fermentation, substrates, kinetics, pH, salt, temperature, oxygen, and endpoint control"}, {"id": "d4892b3f-84b1-4d79-b1e7-f1a606313b87", "type": "concept", "title": "Yogurt, cheese, bread, vegetables, legumes, cocoa/coffee, vinegar, and beverages"}, {"id": "4f13b83a-0c9d-4b69-bf07-2d2a00279aec", "type": "concept", "title": "Probiotics, live cultures, fermented foods, claims, survival, and evidence limitations"}]}]}, {"id": "facb891d-536c-4177-b42c-359ade403e26", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Preservation, Packaging, and Shelf Life", "description": "Shelf life ends when safety, quality, nutrition, or acceptability no longer meets specifications. This section integrates preservation hurdles with packaging and evidence-based shelf-life design.", "children": [{"id": "f923d872-c873-4b3e-8195-725a2200bfdf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Preservation strategies and hurdle design", "description": "Students choose combinations that fit the food, target organisms/reactions, consumer use, and distribution.", "children": [{"id": "3f4825e6-90ba-41fa-94ca-9d6f14030236", "type": "concept", "title": "Temperature control, drying, concentration, acidification, fermentation, preservatives, atmosphere, and thermal/nonthermal processing"}, {"id": "8edd96cb-42f4-472d-9c72-f2a4955ac3cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Synergistic hurdles, process severity, quality tradeoffs, and failure modes"}, {"id": "731323c5-a1a0-4bfa-92b6-b476f1beaf22", "type": "concept", "title": "Home/cultural preservation versus validated commercial processes"}]}, {"id": "00009c8d-721b-4202-aeb6-3ee7da6b12b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Packaging functions and materials", "description": "This subtopic treats packaging as part of the food system, not decoration.", "children": [{"id": "9da9451c-bfd6-45c3-bca1-260418d890e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Containment, protection, communication, convenience, portioning, tamper evidence, and traceability"}, {"id": "20e8de86-7647-4388-ade4-32424441d766", "type": "concept", "title": "Glass, metal, paper/paperboard, polymers, multilayers, coatings, active packaging, and intelligent indicators"}, {"id": "e3b8a1d1-4781-43af-88ed-f2a6fc4c29a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Moisture/oxygen/light/aroma barriers, seal integrity, migration, headspace, and modified atmosphere"}]}, {"id": "5ae8531e-7e5c-4d0f-950f-dc61ec113766", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Shelf-life study design", "description": "Students establish failure criteria and collect data under realistic and accelerated conditions.", "children": [{"id": "a9b2862e-656e-40a2-b64b-e14ca6709f10", "type": "concept", "title": "Safety, microbial, chemical, physical, sensory, nutritional, and package failure endpoints"}, {"id": "79fd22f9-e399-4193-be62-2e168e8dfd38", "type": "concept", "title": "Real-time, accelerated, abuse, opened-package, and distribution studies"}, {"id": "52e46183-3a2b-45cd-b390-b2ff14271180", "type": "concept", "title": "Sampling intervals, replication, kinetic models, uncertainty, date marking, and consumer instructions"}]}, {"id": "a9b6a727-4d8f-44dc-9955-e3e731013c7c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Packaging sustainability and food-waste tradeoffs", "description": "Students avoid one-dimensional judgments by considering product loss, material impacts, reuse/recycling systems, and actual infrastructure.", "children": [{"id": "a06a7e4d-9c85-496b-bfda-b786c6b25368", "type": "concept", "title": "Source reduction, lightweighting, reuse, recyclability, compostability, and renewable content"}, {"id": "00f244d5-adb0-4082-9496-45e0eec99730", "type": "concept", "title": "Life-cycle thinking, functional unit, food loss prevention, transport efficiency, and contamination"}, {"id": "445379f9-32f4-4058-9860-ef7433bfd974", "type": "concept", "title": "Design for recovery, labeling claims, local systems, and unintended consequences"}]}]}, {"id": "65ca26fa-8f88-4311-8282-53b14d0e8ce1", "type": "topic", "title": "Sensory Science, Consumer Testing, and Statistics", "description": "Human perception is a scientific measurement domain. Students learn to select methods, control bias, analyze data, and distinguish product differences from consumer liking.", "children": [{"id": "c4f6a14a-29b5-4bf7-9af8-70843f8dd0c0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sensory perception and test environment", "description": "Students connect appearance, aroma, taste, texture, and chemesthesis to physiology and context.", "children": [{"id": "87e863bf-8b1d-47d6-9b57-1712d5f62c8e", "type": "concept", "title": "Orthonasal/retronasal olfaction, taste, trigeminal sensation, vision, hearing, touch, and oral processing"}, {"id": "e8ab99eb-b318-45bb-8752-eaa57801ab75", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptation, fatigue, carryover, expectation, halo, order, contrast, and context effects"}, {"id": "65582173-1ee2-47ce-b20d-dc7f7517adf3", "type": "concept", "title": "Controlled booths/rooms, lighting, temperature, serving order, coding, palate cleansing, and sample handling"}]}, {"id": "ba0545fb-25e7-404a-a58a-f0c3aba3c949", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Analytical sensory methods", "description": "This subtopic measures whether products differ and how their attributes differ.", "children": [{"id": "f33363dd-500c-4d73-a02e-ffd04eff054e", "type": "concept", "title": "Triangle, duo-trio, paired comparison, tetrad, and threshold concepts"}, {"id": "76d9bf5a-688d-4f67-a811-37ab067b3271", "type": "concept", "title": "Descriptive analysis, panel selection/training, lexicons, references, calibration, and repeatability"}, {"id": "0c1b7a88-a748-4c12-9019-bd0f36ed1e60", "type": "concept", "title": "Difference-from-control, ranking, quality grading, and time-intensity concepts"}]}, {"id": "fc6b500a-5a69-4dac-8f08-086bf3d9123f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Affective and consumer methods", "description": "Students learn what liking and preference can—and cannot—tell product developers and dietetics professionals.", "children": [{"id": "0e0ab5f7-d9ba-4a17-a3c5-78b5f3a19241", "type": "concept", "title": "Hedonic scales, preference, just-about-right, purchase intent, check-all-that-apply, and open response"}, {"id": "51543d43-c2fd-4ebd-bbb7-67366b591c34", "type": "concept", "title": "Target population, recruitment, sample size, central-location/home-use tests, and cultural context"}, {"id": "4a76b405-1767-4a45-8d47-dc105207d703", "type": "concept", "title": "Segmentation, penalties, demand characteristics, ethics, privacy, and generalizability"}]}, {"id": "8a59be8f-676b-40fc-89d6-69e667c5c3e0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Experimental design and statistics", "description": "Students use statistics as a design and reasoning tool rather than a final software button.", "children": [{"id": "dd949e60-319c-4976-8d5e-a700d0e2e3ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Randomization, blocking, counterbalancing, replication, independent units, and repeated measures"}, {"id": "7cd3fe08-8ca0-4092-ad21-aca1e59c7710", "type": "concept", "title": "Descriptive statistics, confidence intervals, t tests, chi-square, ANOVA, post-hoc comparisons, correlation, and regression at an introductory level"}, {"id": "c930d9e1-f647-43e9-9dfd-24d62959d12e", "type": "concept", "title": "Effect size, multiple testing, assumptions, missing data, visualization, and practical versus statistical significance"}]}]}, {"id": "77a1fe82-85a4-40ce-9a89-a24d2ad630d3", "type": "topic", "title": "Nutrition, Processing, Quality, Regulation, and Sustainability", "description": "Food science decisions change nutrient composition, bioaccessibility, safety, cost, acceptability, and environmental impact. This section helps dietetics students evaluate processing without simplistic “processed/unprocessed” binaries.", "children": [{"id": "2c5a7c95-9d60-49ec-a791-e1dbd9572bad", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Processing and nutritional quality", "description": "Students analyze mechanisms and tradeoffs rather than assuming all processing is beneficial or harmful.", "children": [{"id": "dc4e6c4e-901c-4b0c-bb1a-d74c5bd0215d", "type": "concept", "title": "Retention, leaching, oxidation, thermal degradation, matrix disruption, particle size, and bioaccessibility"}, {"id": "7050fdf9-1219-4638-a2ea-ee77eb377234", "type": "concept", "title": "Fortification, enrichment, reformulation, fermentation, germination, extrusion, and cooking"}, {"id": "ae9f8c5a-1c17-410b-84a1-900701a43cb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Digestibility, glycemic response, satiety, sodium/sugar/fat reduction, and sensory compensation"}]}, {"id": "6b67c2f8-7d27-4e13-9a95-e03d35a98094", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Quality assurance and food law", "description": "This subtopic connects specifications and process control to truthful, consistent, lawful products.", "children": [{"id": "c37f421d-e310-4c62-9c8f-efee9cb22163", "type": "concept", "title": "Quality attributes, specifications, tolerance, control charts, acceptance sampling, nonconformance, and corrective action"}, {"id": "07a89b9e-31b4-4bf8-a07a-987b55ee1aee", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity standards, ingredients, additives, allergens, nutrition labeling, claims, net quantity, traceability, and recalls"}, {"id": "6397ad43-24ae-448b-9883-ccc633c5314f", "type": "concept", "title": "Regulatory jurisdiction, validated methods, documentation, and change control"}]}, {"id": "9d1bce1f-c73b-4a20-877a-d3cfc359ecf0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Sustainable food science", "description": "Students evaluate environmental and social consequences with a systems lens.", "children": [{"id": "fcf44347-8f9e-4d1a-a6cc-a9885a33b040", "type": "concept", "title": "Energy, water, emissions, land, biodiversity, labor, by-products, waste, cold chain, and food loss"}, {"id": "035b4c00-c9d9-4ccb-b4d7-41bd71d0c6d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Life-cycle assessment concepts, functional units, boundaries, allocation, tradeoffs, and uncertainty"}, {"id": "bc66972e-a88b-468f-bcd7-069704b21aa0", "type": "concept", "title": "Upcycling, side-stream valorization, circularity, local/global supply, and nutrition-sensitive sustainability"}]}, {"id": "e87c0db9-f70a-4a38-ad80-b22be2dcb24a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Emerging foods and responsible innovation", "description": "Students assess novel ingredients and technologies using evidence, safety, nutrition, function, equity, regulation, and consumer context.", "children": [{"id": "1d581562-7ee9-490f-9455-5e541a6b0ab2", "type": "concept", "title": "Plant-based, precision-fermented, cultivated-cell, insect, algae, novel-protein, and personalized products"}, {"id": "e998c7ad-987c-4881-b3f6-a5cb618fb9da", "type": "concept", "title": "Ultra-processing debates, classification systems, confounding, reformulation, and health-outcome evidence"}, {"id": "caf705c2-8099-437d-a578-466fff1338a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Responsible claims, access, cultural fit, intellectual property, transparency, and unintended consequences"}]}]}, {"id": "65968e15-0c6c-490b-938e-2e48eb4ad712", "type": "topic", "title": "Food Product Development and Capstone Experiment", "description": "The capstone integrates composition, processing, safety, sensory science, nutrition, quality, cost, and sustainability. Students design an experiment—not merely a recipe trial—and defend decisions with evidence.", "children": [{"id": "e13552ca-bc7f-45d1-9c4c-bbd4d6d0cc31", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Problem definition and product brief", "description": "Students identify a real user need and translate it into measurable design requirements.", "children": [{"id": "85cb10e2-5304-41b9-b926-6210f4b4e12b", "type": "concept", "title": "Target user, eating occasion, need state, competitive set, value proposition, and constraints"}, {"id": "24e5c91e-b68b-4392-adfd-35bd2dbd61c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Nutrition, sensory, safety, shelf-life, preparation, packaging, cost, accessibility, and sustainability targets"}, {"id": "59b70e69-e007-4831-bfda-e4a2dc094cc5", "type": "concept", "title": "Literature and patent search, benchmark products, assumptions, and risk register"}]}, {"id": "34f099b0-b5e6-4752-b8f5-d40ef5e743f3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Prototype and experimental design", "description": "This subtopic turns formulation variables into an efficient, interpretable test plan.", "children": [{"id": "79de51be-825e-47b2-987e-096ecf5e7470", "type": "concept", "title": "Control and treatment formulations, factors, levels, responses, and acceptance criteria"}, {"id": "aaa8d473-f77b-4db5-949c-dd2d070b7df9", "type": "concept", "title": "Mass balance, scaling, standardized procedure, process parameters, randomization, replication, and blocking"}, {"id": "f3af0a02-6fe6-43b1-8598-a5e5ef6d05fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Pilot trials, screening, factorial concepts, mixture-design awareness, and iteration"}]}, {"id": "0c3e2d03-dc12-4220-ae10-54586144296f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Measurement and evidence integration", "description": "Students combine instrumental, sensory, nutritional, safety, and process evidence without overstating conclusions.", "children": [{"id": "689c7e35-1164-47be-9580-3723ce61343a", "type": "concept", "title": "Yield, moisture/water activity, pH, color, texture/viscosity, stability, nutrient calculation, and other fit-for-purpose measures"}, {"id": "ec098e00-47fe-4050-a973-9b7092e659be", "type": "concept", "title": "Sensory method selection, consumer limitations, statistical analysis, graphs, uncertainty, and discrepancies"}, {"id": "431a63f7-1967-45a8-beae-5b5b5bf47a7b", "type": "concept", "title": "Ingredient functionality, mechanism, tradeoffs, and decision matrix"}]}, {"id": "78548c0f-715e-40a5-8567-bc29707301c2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technical communication and reproducibility", "description": "Students present a product and scientific argument that another team could audit and repeat.", "children": [{"id": "168d53af-787c-43f6-8ed5-67a31bbc8329", "type": "concept", "title": "Formula, process flow, specifications, critical parameters, equipment, records, and version history"}, {"id": "fb98baef-c3a2-41c8-8232-1c18987c26f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Results, discussion, limitations, cost/nutrition estimate, safety and regulatory considerations, packaging, and shelf-life plan"}, {"id": "a61341fe-144c-4341-9f9f-8dd1e16dc7b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Poster, technical report, oral defense, sample presentation, peer review, and next-step recommendations"}]}]}], "dbBacked": true}$fscijson$::jsonb,
